# Supply-chain security controls — added 2026-08-16.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: this repo is edited around the clock by an autonomous coding
# agent permitted to run `npm install`, executing as the machine owner on a box
# that holds live Stripe keys, the production DATABASE_URL, and the agent's own
# provider credentials. A single malicious or typosquatted dependency running an
# install script there is a total, unrecoverable compromise. Known-CVE gates do
# not help: a fresh typosquat has no advisory on day zero, and node_modules/ is
# gitignored so neither the secret scanner nor the sprint watchdog ever looks.
#
# ── strict-allow-scripts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Install scripts run ONLY for packages explicitly approved in package.json's
# `allowScripts` map, and anything unapproved is a HARD FAILURE rather than a
# silent skip. Deliberately chosen over a blanket `ignore-scripts=true`: that
# would also skip the legitimate ones (Prisma's postinstall fetches the query
# engines), producing a quietly broken build instead of a loud alarm.
#
# The allow-list is VERSION-PINNED (`esbuild@0.21.5`, not `esbuild`), so a
# version bump forces a fresh human approval — review-on-bump, for free.
# Approve a new one deliberately with:  npm install-scripts approve <pkg>
# Never approve one to "make the build work" without reading what it does.
#
# ── min-release-age ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 7 DAYS of cooldown before npm will resolve a newly published version.
# NOTE THE UNIT: this is days, not minutes — verified empirically, because
# assuming minutes would have shipped a control that does nothing.
# Turns "malicious version published at hour 0, yanked at hour ~30" into a
# non-event. No scanner catches an hour-0 publish; only time does.
# `npm ci` is unaffected (it installs the lockfile verbatim).
#
# Both are reversible by deleting the relevant line.
strict-allow-scripts=true
min-release-age=7
